---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import Draft from '../../components/Draft.astro';
import { entity } from '../../lib/entity';

// The day each row's transfer basis was last read on the company's own
// pages (its DPA and its statement of certification under the EU-U.S. Data
// Privacy Framework). Change it when the rows are checked again.
const checked = '2026-11-01';
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{ title: 'Subprocessors', description: 'Who processes data for version.cam, for what, where, and on what basis.' }}
>
	<Draft />
	<p class="vc-lead">
		The companies that process data for version.cam, what each one does, where, and on what basis personal data
		may leave the European Union. Last checked on {checked}, against each company's data processing agreement and
		its own statement of certification under the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework (DPF).
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Company</th>
				<th>What for</th>
				<th>Where</th>
				<th>Transfer basis</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>Cloudflare, Inc., United States</td>
				<td>
					Hosting the pages and the API; the database; the storage of recordings and renders; the queues; the
					containers that render; the request logs; Turnstile on the services form; the routing of our e-mail
					addresses
				</td>
				<td>
					Storage in Cloudflare's EU jurisdiction; the network worldwide; while a version renders, its screens are held in the rendering container for the seconds the render
					takes, wherever that container runs
				</td>
				<td>
					<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-customer-dpa/">Cloudflare's customer DPA</a>; EU-U.S.
					DPF, certified; the EU standard contractual clauses in the DPA should the certification lapse
				</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>GitHub, Inc., United States</td>
				<td>Signing in; the App's check runs, comments and issues on your repositories</td>
				<td>United States</td>
				<td>
					<a href="https://github.com/customer-terms/github-data-protection-agreement">GitHub's Data Protection
					Agreement</a>; EU-U.S. DPF, certified; the EU standard contractual clauses otherwise
				</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Resend, Inc., United States</td>
				<td>E-mail: notifications about clips, notices about the beta and billing, and the services form's messages</td>
				<td>Sent from Resend's EU region; the company and its logs are in the United States</td>
				<td>
					<a href="https://resend.com/legal/dpa">Resend's DPA</a>; EU-U.S. DPF, certified; the EU standard
					contractual clauses in the DPA
				</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Stripe Payments Europe, Ltd., Ireland</td>
				<td>Payments, invoices and tax, once payment opens</td>
				<td>Ireland, with Stripe's own processors; Stripe, LLC in the United States</td>
				<td>
					<a href="https://stripe.com/legal/dpa">Stripe's DPA</a> with its data transfers addendum; EU-U.S. DPF,
					certified, for the United States
				</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2>Who receives data without processing it for us</h2>
	<p>
		GitHub receives the App's check runs, comments and issues on your repositories, on your project's
		instruction. Slack receives what your project's webhook says, if you gave it one. Nobody else receives data: even the badge on the front
		page is drawn by our own API, not by another service.
	</p>

	<h2>Changes</h2>
	<p>
		We tell customers by e-mail, to the address we have for the account, 30 days before adding a company to this
		list or widening what one does. You may object within those 30 days; if we cannot meet the objection, you may
		end the agreement, and the unused part of a paid period is refunded. Questions:{' '}
		<a href={`mailto:${entity.contactEmail}`}>{entity.contactEmail}</a>.
	</p>
</StarlightPage>
